{"n": 1, "grade": "wrong-relation", "reason": "The paper says \"Deligne developed a general theory for gluing two pseudofunctors of target Cat_1 [6] Section 3\" and that \"The construction of F thus amounts to gluing the two pseudofunctors\"; this applies the theory to F and does not say a special case is obtained by fixing parameters.", "doubt": true, "doubt_reason": "Applying a general theory to one construction could be read as specialisation; the rubric does not clearly decide whether applying a theory counts as generalises."}
{"n": 2, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Corollary 0.4: \"Then the natural map δ*_2 N(Sch'')^cart_{P,I} → N(Sch'') is a categorical equivalence\", with P the proper morphisms and I \"the subset of local isomorphisms\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 3, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Theorem 0.1: \"Let C be a category admitting pullbacks ... satisfying the following conditions ... Then the natural map (0.9) g ... is a categorical equivalence (Definition 1.7).\"", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 4, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Proof of Corollary 0.3: \"This follows immediately from Corollary 0.2 applied to C=Sch', E1=P, E2=J\", so Corollary 0.3 is Corollary 0.2 with its parameters fixed (rewritten as generalises(Corollary 0.2, Corollary 0.3)).", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 5, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Lemma 2.4(1): \"the simplicial set Γ_Φ(R) is nonempty and connected, hence a connected component of Γ(R)\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 6, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Corollary 0.4: \"Then the natural map δ*_2 N(Sch'')^cart_{P,I} → N(Sch'') is a categorical equivalence.\"", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 7, "grade": "wrong-modality", "reason": "This paper asserts the equivalence itself: \"Let us mention in passing that there exists a canonical categorical equivalence from the simplicial set δ*_{2,{2}} C^cart_{E1,E2} to the ∞-category of correspondences introduced in [9] (Example 4.30)\"; only the correspondence category comes from [9].", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 8, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "\"This follows immediately from Corollary 0.2 applied to C=Sch', E1=P, E2=J\" appears in Corollary 0.3's proof, so Corollary 0.3 is a special case of Corollary 0.2.", "doubt": true, "doubt_reason": "The specialisation is asserted in the proof of Corollary 0.3; it is unclear whether the bare locator \"Corollary 0.3\" covers its proof."}
{"n": 9, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "\"Taking k=2 and E_0=Ar(C) we obtain the following. Corollary 0.2.\" This matches rubric example generalises(Theorem 0.1, Corollary 0.2).", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 10, "grade": "wrong-relation", "reason": "The header reads \"Lemma 2.5 ( [ 12 ] *Lemma 3.1.3)\", which is an attribution, and the rubric says \"`cites` is not a relation type -- modality, never a relation type\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 11, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "\"Let us illustrate them in the case of Corollary 0.2. The map g can be decomposed as ... g' ... g'' ... We prove that both g' and g'' are categorical equivalences.\" The factorisation and the two equivalences are steps of the proof route.", "doubt": true, "doubt_reason": "The passage is an illustration of Theorem 0.1's proof strategy, and Corollary 0.2 is formally obtained from Theorem 0.1, so it is unclear whether this counts as Corollary 0.2's proof."}
{"n": 12, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Lemma 2.4(2): \"For homotopic morphisms Φ, Φ': N → R, we have Γ_Φ(R) = Γ_Φ'(R).\"", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 13, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "\"The fact that g'' is a categorical equivalence is an ∞-categorical generalization of Deligne's result [6] *Proposition 3.3.2.\"", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 14, "grade": "wrong-relation", "reason": "\"This follows immediately from Corollary 0.2 applied to C=Sch', E1=P, E2=J\" makes Corollary 0.3 an instance of Corollary 0.2, and the rubric's boundary says \"a special case doesn't use the general result; it is an instance of it\" (generalises).", "doubt": true, "doubt_reason": "The proof does invoke Corollary 0.2 as its only step, so the depends-on admissibility test also passes; only the generalises boundary rule decides this."}
{"n": 15, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Definition 1.7: \"A map f: T → S of simplicial sets is a categorical equivalence if for every ∞-category C, the induced functor hFun(S,C) → hFun(T,C) is an equivalence of ordinary categories.\"", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 16, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "\"we need an enhancement of the pseudofunctor F above. In the language of ∞-categories [20], such an enhancement is a functor F^∞: N(Sch') → Cat_∞\", whose homotopy category \"is equivalent to D(X,Λ)\" and with \"hf_!^∞ ... equivalent to the classical f_!\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 17, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "\"The functoriality of this operation is encoded by a pseudofunctor F: Sch' → Cat_1 sending a scheme X in Sch' to D(X,Λ) and a morphism f: Y → X in Sch' to the functor f_!\" (first use of F).", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 18, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "\"The fact that g'' is a categorical equivalence is an ∞-categorical generalization of Deligne's result [6] *Proposition 3.3.2.\"", "doubt": true, "doubt_reason": "The subject is the map g'' itself, not the result that g'' is a categorical equivalence; it is unclear whether this mis-kinding makes the relation wrong."}
